ndling but interpersonal issues and memory. C has strong situational awareness and leadership but attitude issues. A has reliability and crew atmosphere but communication issues. D has problem solving and responsibility but arrogance and weak leadership.\n\nWe need to consider which concerns are more manageable. B's grumpy/uncooperative and memory for numbers could be mitigated? Memory for numbers could be a safety risk for flight calculations. Might be mitigated with checklists, but still concerning. C's not open to new ideas could affect adaptability and feedback orientation, which is important for safety. A's communication difficulty could affect crew coordination, especially under pressure. D's arrogance and hot temper could cause crew friction.\n\nGiven the safety-critical nature, crew cooperation and communication are crucial. B's grumpy/uncooperative is a direct problem. C's attitude also problematic. A's communication difficulty is also direct. D's arrogance/hot temper also direct.\n\nWhich candidate has the least severe cooperation/communication issue? A creates positive crew atmosphere, but difficulty communicating ideas and egocentric may cause issues. B's grumpy/uncooperative may be more severe. C's show-off and not open to new ideas may be severe. D's arrogance/hot temper also severe.\n\nThus maybe A's positive atmosphere may offset communication difficulty? But egocentric and not willing to further education may affect adaptability.\n\nWe need to decide. I think B's memory issue is a concrete safety risk. C's attitude may be less immediate safety risk but could affect crew. A's communication difficulty could be serious under pressure.\n\nGiven the panel's earlier leaning to B, but we have concerns about memory for numbers. Do we have any evidence about B's memory for numbers? Yes, \"relatively weak memory for numbers\". That could affect flight planning, fuel calculations, etc. That is a serious operational risk.\n\nC has no memory issues noted. C's leadership is very good, situational awareness strong. Attitude concerns: show-off, not open to new ideas, unfriendly. Could be mitigated with coaching? Possibly.\n\nThus maybe C is stronger overall than B, because technical and leadership strengths outweigh attitude concerns, while B's memory issue is a concrete safety risk.\n\nThus I might stick with Candidate C. I need to argue that despite attitude concerns, C's situational awareness, leadership, and technical competence are critical, and the attitude issues are less directly safety-critical than B's memory weakness and grumpy behavior.\n\nThus I will keep my vote for Candidate C, and explain why.\n\nWe need to reference discussion: Sofia argued B is best, Anna gave D's temperament, A's reliability. I will respond: I still think C is best because of anticipatory skills, leadership, and no memory issues, and attitude concerns can be mitigated.\n\nThus produce PUBLIC_MESSAGE and METADATA_JSON with vote \"Candidate C\"."}
